Package: hd-base
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: all
Maintainer: Erik Van Hamme <erik.vanhamme@gmail.com>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: python3, python3-jinja2, sudo, kbd, systemd, systemd-sysv, udev, kmod,
 openssl, efitools, sbsigntool,
 openssh-server, openssh-client, kitty-terminfo, ufw,
 iproute2, iputils-ping, netbase, linux-sysctl-defaults,
 zfs-dkms, zfsutils-linux, zfs-initramfs, zfs-zed,
 initramfs-tools, zstd,
 efibootmgr,
 locales, keyboard-configuration, console-setup, ca-certificates,
 systemd-resolved, libpam-systemd, libnss-systemd,
 nano, less, procps,
 eza, fzf, psmisc, bash-completion, command-not-found, man-db, apt-file,
 systemd-zram-generator
Conflicts: zram-tools, hd-config, hd-network, hd-secboot
Replaces: hd-config, hd-network, hd-secboot
Provides: hd-config, hd-network, hd-secboot
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Description: HyprDebian base system
 Depends on the packages that every hyprdebian system has, so that they are
 installed with it and stay installed: systemd as init, the SSH server and
 client and the firewall, the network tools, ZFS with its event daemon and
 DKMS to build its module, the console, name resolution, the login sessions
 of systemd, the Secure Boot tools, and the command line tools. It keeps the
 system small: dpkg leaves out the documentation but the copyright files,
 the translations and the man pages in other languages than English, and apt
 downloads no translations of the package descriptions. Monthly timers scrub
 and trim the ZFS pools.
 .
 It also installs hd-firmware, which finds and installs the firmware packages
 that the hardware needs, hd-up, which upgrades the system after a ZFS snapshot,
 hd-reboot and hd-poweroff, which work on a desktop and without one,
 and the files that new users get in their home directory: aliases for eza,
 fzf, yazi and ffmpeg, and a nano config, with hd-sync-skel,
 which brings them into an existing home directory. It adds ~/.local/bin to
 the PATH, keeps ifupdown, os-prober and turnstile from being installed, and
 turns Num Lock on at the console login. It sets up the zram swap, the
 resolver without a stub, masks the units HyprDebian does not use, and
 points /etc/resolv.conf at the name servers of systemd-resolved.
 After a login it shows the HyprDebian banner and the state of the system:
 the pools, the RAID arrays and the failed units. Above the login prompt it
 shows the version and the suite, written in
 /etc/issue by hd-issue. hd-link-repo switches the HyprDebian repository and
 suite that apt gets the hd-* packages from.
 .
 It keeps the hyprdebian configuration in /etc/hyprdebian/config.json, where
 hyprdebian packages write their settings under a key of their own with
 hd-config-update and hd-config-remove. It renders the systemd-networkd
 files from the key hd-network with hd-network-render; hd-network-iface and
 hd-network-bridge change the interfaces and bridges in it. It names
 ethernet and wifi interfaces by their path, such as enp3s0, also in the
 initramfs.
 .
 On its first installation it generates the Secure Boot keys of the owner in
 /etc/hyprdebian/secboot: PK, KEK and db, each with its certificate, EFI
 signature list and signed update. Existing keys are never replaced.
 hd-secboot-enroll enrolls them in the firmware, and hd-secboot-check checks
 that Secure Boot is on.
